  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "English has the verb **fear**, but everyday speech mostly prefers **be afraid**; many languages use only a verb here, so look for the natural verb form when one exists. The polite phrase *I'm afraid* carries no fear at all and needs a formula for regret.",
    "grammar": "**Afraid** is followed by **of** plus a noun or an *-ing* form, by **to** plus a verb, or by a *that*-clause: *afraid of dogs*, *afraid of falling*, *afraid to ask*, *afraid that it will rain*. It needs **be**, and never comes before a noun.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "The stress is on the second syllable; the first vowel is weak, like the **a** of **about**."
  },
